      "Someone searching 'near me' on Google, or opening Google Maps to find a business nearby, is usually much closer to making a decision than someone scrolling social media. That's what makes local search — and specifically, your Google Business Profile — worth taking seriously.",
      "A complete, accurate, well-categorized Google Business Profile affects whether your business appears in the local map results at all, and whether it looks credible once someone finds it. That means an accurate category, complete business information, consistent details that match your website, and profile photos that represent the actual business.",
      "Local SEO also depends on consistency beyond the profile itself — the same business name, address format and phone number should appear the same way everywhere your business is listed online. Inconsistencies, even small ones, can weaken how confidently Google associates your business with a location.",
      "It's worth being clear that Google Business Profile verification is governed entirely by Google's own policies, and eligibility depends on your business type and location — this isn't something any agency can guarantee or bypass. What AMREN can do is make sure the profile itself is set up correctly and optimized within those requirements, and that it's supported by consistent local SEO signals across your website.",
      "## How Google ranks local results",
      "Google describes three main factors for local results: relevance (how well a profile matches what someone searched for), distance (how far the business is from the searcher or the location in the search) and prominence (how well known the business is, informed by things like reviews, links and overall web presence). You can't change distance, but relevance and prominence are both within your control.",
      "## A Google Business Profile checklist",
      "- Primary category: choose the most specific category that describes your core business. It's one of the strongest relevance signals you control.\n- Secondary categories: add only those that genuinely apply.\n- Business name: use your real-world business name exactly as it appears on signage. Adding keywords to the name is against Google's guidelines and can lead to suspension.\n- Address or service area: show an address only if customers visit you there; otherwise set a service area covering the parts of the UAE you actually serve.\n- Phone and website: use a local number and link to the most relevant page on your site.\n- Hours: keep regular hours accurate and add special hours for Ramadan, Eid and public holidays.\n- Services and products: list each service with a short plain-language description.\n- Photos: add real photos of the premises, team and work, and keep adding new ones.\n- Description: explain what you do, who you serve and where, in natural language.",
      "## Reviews: the part most businesses neglect",
      "Reviews influence both how prominent your profile is and whether someone chooses you once they find it. The most reliable approach is a simple, consistent habit: ask every satisfied customer, make it easy with a direct review link (a WhatsApp message after the service works well in the UAE), and reply to every review — positive or negative — politely and specifically.",
      "Never buy reviews, offer incentives for reviews or post reviews on your own behalf. These practices break Google's policies and can result in reviews being removed or the profile being restricted.",
      "## Supporting your profile from your website",
      "Your website should reinforce what your profile says. Make sure the business name, address and phone number on the site match the profile exactly, add a clear location or contact page, and create a dedicated page for each core service. If you serve several areas — for example Dubai Marina, Business Bay and Al Barsha — mention them naturally where it's genuinely relevant, rather than creating near-identical pages for each area.",
